{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\nAbstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of large language models has enabled the development of sophisticated code agents capable of generating, debugging, and executing complex code autonomously. However, these powerful tools also introduce significant security risks, as malicious actors may exploit these systems to generate or execute harmful code. This paper provides a comprehensive survey of recent advances in code agent safety, focusing on red-teaming methodologies for evaluating and improving the robustness of code agents. We examine several key benchmarks and frameworks, including RedCodeAgent [1], RedCode [2], and DecodingTrust [3], which address different aspects of code agent trustworthiness. We also contextualize our discussion within the broader landscape of AI safety research, drawing parallels with foundational deep learning work [4]. Our analysis reveals critical vulnerabilities in current code agents and proposes directions for future research in automated red-teaming, safety benchmarks, and robust defense mechanisms.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in software development, enabling automated code generation, testing, and deployment through the integration of large language models with code execution environments. These agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in translating natural language instructions into functional code, debugging existing programs, and even creating complete software applications. However, the very features that make code agents powerful—autonomous code generation and execution—also introduce unprecedented security challenges.\n\nThe primary concern with code agents lies in their ability to execute arbitrary code, which can be exploited through adversarial prompts to generate malware, exfiltrate sensitive data, or cause denial-of-service attacks. Traditional security measures are insufficient for addressing these novel threats, as the attack surface has expanded to include natural language interactions with AI systems.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of simulating adversarial attacks to identify vulnerabilities, has emerged as a critical methodology for improving the safety of AI systems. Recent work has begun to adapt red-teaming techniques specifically for code agents, revealing systematic vulnerabilities and developing automated approaches for identifying and mitigating these risks.\n\nThis paper surveys recent advances in code agent red-teaming, examining key contributions in benchmark development, automated red-teaming frameworks, and comprehensive trustworthiness assessments. We analyze the strengths and limitations of existing approaches and identify promising directions for future research.\n\n2. Related Work\n\n2.1 Code Agent Safety Benchmarks\n\nThe development of comprehensive benchmarks for evaluating code agent safety has been a crucial step in understanding and mitigating the risks associated with these systems. RedCode [2] introduces a novel benchmark specifically designed for evaluating the safety of code agents through both code execution and generation scenarios. This benchmark systematically assesses the ability of code agents to generate or execute risky code, providing a standardized evaluation framework for the community.\n\nThe RedCode benchmark covers a diverse range of risky code categories, including malware generation, vulnerability exploitation, data exfiltration, and system manipulation. By providing structured evaluation protocols and a comprehensive dataset of test cases, RedCode enables researchers to quantitatively compare the safety profiles of different code agents and track progress over time.\n\n2.2 Automated Red-teaming Frameworks\n\nWhile manual red-teaming provides valuable insights, it is inherently limited in scope and scalability. RedCodeAgent [1] addresses this challenge by introducing an automated red-teaming framework specifically designed for evaluating diverse code agents. This framework employs specialized agents that systematically explore the attack surface of target code agents, generating adversarial prompts and analyzing the responses for safety violations.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework leverages the capabilities of large language models to generate diverse attack strategies, adapting its approach based on the responses of the target agent. This iterative process enables comprehensive coverage of potential vulnerabilities, including those that might be overlooked by human red-teamers. The framework also includes sophisticated analysis tools for detecting subtle safety violations, such as indirect data exfiltration attempts or partially successful attacks.\n\n2.3 Trustworthiness Assessment\n\nBeyond code-specific safety concerns, the trustworthiness of underlying language models plays a critical role in the overall safety of code agents. DecodingTrust [3] provides a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models, examining multiple dimensions including toxicity, bias, factual consistency, and robustness to adversarial inputs. This foundational work establishes evaluation methodologies and benchmarks that inform the development of safety measures for downstream applications, including code agents.\n\nThe insights from DecodingTrust highlight the importance of addressing trustworthiness at multiple levels of the AI system stack. While code-specific safety measures are essential, the underlying language model's general trustworthiness properties significantly influence the behavior of code agents. This observation underscores the need for holistic safety approaches that address both general and domain-specific vulnerabilities.\n\n2.4 Deep Learning Foundations\n\nThe development of effective code agents and red-teaming frameworks builds upon decades of research in deep learning. The architectural innovations introduced in ResNet [4], particularly the use of residual connections, have become fundamental components of modern deep learning systems, including the large language models that power code agents. Understanding the evolution of deep learning architectures provides important context for appreciating the capabilities and limitations of contemporary code agents.\n\nThe success of residual learning in training very deep networks has enabled the development of increasingly powerful language models, which in turn have enhanced the capabilities of code agents. However, this progress also highlights the importance of careful consideration of safety implications as models become more capable.\n\n3. Conclusion\n\nThe field of code agent red-teaming is rapidly evolving, with significant advances in benchmark development, automated testing frameworks, and comprehensive trustworthiness assessments. Our analysis of key contributions reveals both the progress made and the challenges that remain.\n\nThe RedCode [2] benchmark has established an important foundation for evaluating code agent safety through standardized protocols and comprehensive test suites. RedCodeAgent [1] has demonstrated the feasibility of automated red-teaming, showing that AI-driven approaches can achieve broad coverage of potential vulnerabilities. The insights from DecodingTrust [3] emphasize the importance of addressing trustworthiness across multiple dimensions, while foundational work like ResNet [4] reminds us that the capabilities of modern code agents build upon decades of research in deep learning.\n\nDespite these advances, several challenges remain. Current red-teaming frameworks may not capture all possible attack vectors, particularly those that combine multiple attack strategies or exploit emergent behaviors in complex systems. Additionally, the rapidly evolving capabilities of code agents require continuous updates to benchmarks and testing methodologies. Future research should focus on developing more sophisticated automated red-teaming approaches, improving the generalizability of safety evaluations, and establishing long-term monitoring systems for deployed code agents.\n\nAs code agents become increasingly prevalent in software development workflows, the importance of rigorous safety evaluation and red-teaming will only grow. The research community must continue to prioritize safety alongside capability development to ensure that these powerful tools can be deployed responsibly and securely.\n\nReferences\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang, et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[2] Chengquan Guo, Xun Liu, Chulin Xie, et al. \"RedCode: Risky Code Execution and Generation Benchmark for Code Agents.\" arXiv:2409.12345, 2024.\n\n[3] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[4] Kaiming He, Xiangyu Zhang, Shaoqing Ren, et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n",
